suming weight 1 per requirement, which means I can fit 4 requirements per task maximum.\n\nLet me map out the four tasks with this constraint in mind. Task 0 would be the testutils contract, which creates the Go stub with the necessary types and interfaces.\n\nFor the contract task specifically, I'm thinking about what requirements it should cover. A contract task defines the structural types rather than behavior, so it would map to the type definition requirements from srd001-testutils. Looking at the requirement groups, the R1.x group likely covers the DiffTest struct and test configuration, which is what a contract task would define.\n\nFrom a practical standpoint, since pkg/testutils and pkg/sys are foundational with no interdependencies, I should run both contract tasks in parallel as the starting point, then have their respective implementation tasks depend on those contracts.\n\nThe challenge is that I have the requirement IDs and statuses from requirement_states, plus the exported types from package_contracts, but the actual requirement text isn't provided in the project context. For the contract tasks, I can derive what needs to be defined from the package exports themselves. For implementation tasks, I'll need to reference the SRD requirement IDs even without seeing their full text, since the constraint prevents me from reading files or exploring the filesystem.\n\nLet me map out the contract task for testutils based on what's exported: DiffTest struct, NormalizeFunc type, RunDiffTests and ComposeNormalizers functions, TimestampNormalizer variable, and BuildBinary which appears to be needed for the tests even if it's not explicitly listed in the contract exports. be defined elsewhere or maybe it's not exported from the contract. The CLAUDE.md references it in the differential testing pattern, but the package_contracts section doesn't list it as an export. Since I'm working on a contract task, I need to define all the type signatures that are actually part of the public API. Let me focus on what's explicitly in the contract and structure the requirements around those core exports, keeping in mind I can only have up to 4 requirements per task.\n\nFor the contract tasks, I'm defining Go stubs for the key types and functions. Task 0 covers the testutils contract with DiffTest, NormalizeFunc, and the related functions. Task 1 handles the sys contract with FileInfo, Stat, Lstat, and terminal-related functions. Task 2 then moves into the actual implementation of the testutils requirements, starting with the struct fields and basic RunDiffTests logic.\n\nNow I'm realizing the contract tasks don't directly align with the SRD requirement IDs, so I need to map out what R1.1-R1.4 actually covers. Looking at the srd001 structure, R1 has 4 requirements that likely correspond to the DiffTest struct definition, while R2 probably covers RunDiffTests, R3 handles the comparison and normalization logic, R4 covers BuildBinary, and R5 covers utilities. Task 3 will then implement the sys contract starting with FileInfo and the Stat/Lstat functions.\n\nI'm overcomplicating the contract task mapping—the constraint is clear that the contract should define all exported types and function signatures as stubs with a dependency of -1, and then implementation tasks fill in the actual behavior. Rather than trying to anchor the contract to specific SRD IDs, I should just reference the requirement groups it covers and move forward with writing out the actual tasks while being mindful of the weight budget constraints. I need to clarify what counts as SRD sub-requirements for the contract tasks—they define all the types across the package, which spans multiple requirement groups. For naming, the constraint requires using the primary exported type name rather than the package name, so pkg/testutils would use difftest.go and pkg/sys would use fileinfo.go. I'm realizing the existing structure might already have some of these files, so I should check what'