difftest.go, and for sys, FileInfo goes in fileinfo.go. The other functions like Stat, Lstat, and TerminalWidth might need their own files depending on how they're organized.\n\nFor the contract phase, I should keep everything in one stub file to define the platform-independent API, though the actual implementation might split into platform-specific files like fileinfo_darwin.go and fileinfo_linux.go since they wrap syscalls. I'm also noticing that BuildBinary appears in the shared_protocols but isn't listed in the package_contracts exports, so I'll stick with what's explicitly defined in the contract rather than adding it based on the architecture alone. I'm realizing the contract task should include all exports that the SRD specifies, including BuildBinary if it's mentioned there, so I'll tell the stitch agent to read the SRD for completeness rather than trying to guess. Now I need to finalize the task ordering—both contracts should come before both implementations since they're independent of each other.\n\nFor the file naming, I should stick with the convention of using the primary export as the filename, so `difftest.go` for testutils and `fileinfo.go` for sys, though sys will likely need platform-specific build tags for its terminal and signal functions. For Go's build tag system, I can't define the same function in both a regular file and a platform-specific file, so the contract needs to put type definitions and function stubs in a single file that implementation tasks will later split into platform-specific files. I need to verify whether go.mod already exists in the repo since it was reset to specs-only—if it doesn't have the `golang.org/x/sys` dependency, the implementation task should note that the stitch agent needs to run `go get` to add it.\n\nLooking at the requirement counts for the two main tasks, I'm reconsidering how to split the work between them.\n\nWith testutils having 22 requirements and sys having 16, breaking them into 4-requirement chunks